\section{Probe Entry Aerothermodynamics}
\label{sec:probe-entry-aerothermodynamics}

\subsection{Overview}
The UAP-01 atmospheric probe enters Uranus’s upper atmosphere at
hyperbolic velocity, encountering extreme aerothermodynamic conditions
including hypersonic shock formation, radiative heating, chemical
nonequilibrium, and ablation-driven thermal protection. This chapter
derives the governing equations for entry heating, shock-layer
thermochemistry, deceleration, and parachute deployment.

\subsection{Entry Conditions}
Entry velocity:
\[
v_{\text{entry}} \approx 22.4~\mathrm{km/s}.
\]

Atmospheric composition (UUAS THERMO-Q2):
\[
\text{H}_2,\; \text{He},\; \text{CH}_4,\; \text{NH}_3,\; \text{C}_x\text{H}_y.
\]

Entry flight path angle:
\[
\gamma \approx -18^\circ.
\]

\subsection{Shock-Layer Formation}
The bow shock forms at standoff distance $d_s$:
\[
d_s \approx \frac{k R_n}{M_\infty^2},
\]
where:
\[
R_n = \text{nose radius}, \quad
M_\infty = \text{freestream Mach number}, \quad
k = \text{empirical constant}.
\]

Shock temperature:
\[
T_2 \approx T_1 \left[
1 + \frac{2(\gamma - 1)}{(\gamma + 1)^2} M_\infty^2
\right],
\]
with $\gamma$ the specific heat ratio.

For Uranus entry:
\[
M_\infty \approx 120,
\qquad
T_2 \sim 10{,}000~\mathrm{K}.
\]

\subsection{Convective Heating}
Stagnation-point convective heat flux:
\[
q_{\text{conv}} = \rho_\infty^{0.5} v_{\text{entry}}^3
\sqrt{\frac{C_h}{R_n}},
\]
where:
\[
C_h = \text{heat transfer coefficient}.
\]

For Uranus entry:
\[
q_{\text{conv}} \sim 8{-}12~\mathrm{MW/m^2}.
\]

\subsection{Radiative Heating}
Radiative heating dominates due to high shock temperatures:
\[
q_{\text{rad}} = \sigma T_2^4 \, \epsilon_{\text{eff}},
\]
where:
\[
\sigma = \text{Stefan-Boltzmann constant}, \quad
\epsilon_{\text{eff}} = \text{effective emissivity}.
\]

For Uranus entry:
\[
q_{\text{rad}} \sim 15{-}25~\mathrm{MW/m^2}.
\]

Total heating:
\[
q_{\text{total}} = q_{\text{conv}} + q_{\text{rad}}
\sim 25{-}35~\mathrm{MW/m^2}.
\]

\subsection{Ablation and Thermal Protection}
The PICA-15 heat shield ablates according to:
\[
\dot{m}_{\text{abl}} = \frac{q_{\text{total}}}{H_{\text{vap}}},
\]
where $H_{\text{vap}}$ is effective enthalpy of ablation.

Recession rate:
\[
\dot{r} = \frac{\dot{m}_{\text{abl}}}{\rho_{\text{PICA}}},
\]
with $\rho_{\text{PICA}}$ the material density.

UUAS THERMO-Q2 predicts:
\[
\dot{r} \approx 0.3{-}0.5~\mathrm{mm/s}
\]
during peak heating.

\subsection{Deceleration Dynamics}
Deceleration force:
\[
D = \frac{1}{2} \rho v^2 C_D A,
\]
where:
\[
C_D = \text{drag coefficient}, \quad
A = \text{reference area}.
\]

Deceleration:
\[
a_D = \frac{D}{m}.
\]

Peak deceleration:
\[
a_D \approx 120{-}150~g.
\]

Velocity decay:
\[
\frac{dv}{dt} = -\frac{1}{2m} \rho v^2 C_D A.
\]

\subsection{Parachute Deployment}
Parachute deployment occurs when:
\[
q = \frac{1}{2} \rho v^2 < q_{\text{max}},
\]
with:
\[
q_{\text{max}} \approx 8{-}12~\mathrm{kPa}.
\]

Deployment altitude:
\[
h_{\text{deploy}} \approx 250{-}300~\mathrm{km},
\]
based on UUAS atmospheric density profiles.

\subsection{Thermochemical Nonequilibrium}
Shock-layer chemistry includes:
\[
\text{H}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{H}, \qquad
\text{CH}_4 \rightarrow \text{C}_x + \text{H}_2,
\qquad
\text{H}_3^+ + e^- \rightarrow \text{H}_2 + \text{H}.
\]

Energy equation:
\[
\frac{dT}{dt}
= \frac{q_{\text{total}} - q_{\text{chem}} - q_{\text{rad}}}{m c_p},
\]
where $q_{\text{chem}}$ is chemical energy absorption.

UUAS ION-Q4 provides:
\[
q_{\text{chem}} \approx 3{-}5~\mathrm{MW/m^2}.
\]

\subsection{Descent Thermal Balance}
Post-shock descent governed by:
\[
m c_p \frac{dT}{dt}
= q_{\text{conv}} - q_{\text{rad}} - q_{\text{abl}}.
\]

Temperature stabilizes when:
\[
q_{\text{conv}} \approx q_{\text{rad}} + q_{\text{abl}}.
\]

\subsection{Conclusion}
The UAP-01 atmospheric probe experiences peak heating of
$25{-}35~\mathrm{MW/m^2}$, shock temperatures near $10{,}000~\mathrm{K}$,
and peak deceleration of $120{-}150~g$. PICA-15 ablation maintains
thermal stability, while parachute deployment at $\sim 275~\mathrm{km}$
ensures controlled descent. These aerothermodynamic derivations confirm
the feasibility of the probe entry architecture and align with UUAS
multi-scale atmospheric and thermochemical models.